window.docContents[41].resolve({"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___2___-Indecomposable-representations-of-the-quivers-___A_1______-___A_2______-___A_3___/Remark-6___2___1___-Notation-for-dimension-diagrams/#Chapter6___Remark6___2___1":{"contents":"Remark 6.2.1. By an object of the type 1 \\xrightarrow{} 0 we mean a map from a 1-dimensional vector space to the zero space. Similarly, an object of the type 0 \\xrightarrow{} 1 is a map from the zero space into a 1-dimensional space. The object 1 \\xrightarrow{} 1 means an isomorphism from a 1-dimensional to another 1-dimensional space. The numbers in such diagrams always mean the dimension of the attached spaces and the maps are the canonical maps (unless specified otherwise).\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t6. Quiver representations\t6.2. Indecomposable representations of the quivers $A_1$, $A_2$, $A_3$\tRemark 6.2.1: Notation for dimension diagrams","header":"Remark 6.2.1: Notation for dimension diagrams","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___2___-Indecomposable-representations-of-the-quivers-___A_1______-___A_2______-___A_3___/Remark-6___2___1___-Notation-for-dimension-diagrams/#Chapter6___Remark6___2___1"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Left-and-right-modules-over-commutative-rings/#Chapter2___Remark2___3___2":{"contents":"Remark 2.3.2. Let M be a left module over a commutative ring A. Then one can regard M as a right A-module, with ma := am. Similarly, any right A-module can be regarded as a left A-module. For this reason, for commutative rings one does not distinguish between left and right A-modules and just calls them A-modules.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t2. Basic notions of representation theory\t2.3. Representations\tLeft and right modules over commutative rings","header":"Left and right modules over commutative rings","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Left-and-right-modules-over-commutative-rings/#Chapter2___Remark2___3___2"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___9___-Exact-functors/Additive-and-k-linear-functor/#Chapter7___Definition7___9___1":{"contents":"Definition 7.9.1. A functor F between two abelian categories is additive if it induces homomorphisms on Hom groups. Also, for k-linear categories one says that F is k-linear if it induces k-linear maps between Hom spaces.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t7. Introduction to categories\t7.9. Exact functors\tAdditive and k-linear functor","header":"Additive and k-linear functor","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___9___-Exact-functors/Additive-and-k-linear-functor/#Chapter7___Definition7___9___1"}});
